Camera: the phone's camera becomes the device's

The A4's camera ISP has always been modelled well enough for the guest to
drive it -- it draws a test pattern, on time, into the buffer the kext gave it,
and both cameras' drivers accept it. What it never had was a picture. This is
that picture, written as the smallest thing that can be: one mailbox between the
app's camera thread and the emulated ISP.

  hw/arm/host-camera.c            one mailbox, not one per sensor and not a
                                  queue. A write copies the phone's newest frame
                                  and, if nobody drew the last one, counts it as
                                  replaced -- a viewfinder drops, it does not
                                  queue, so a slow guest costs a dropped frame
                                  and never a growing buffer. The ISP copies it
                                  out once per frame and holds it until the app
                                  flushes it, so a picture never blinks back to
                                  the test pattern. Sizes that cannot be a
                                  picture are refused rather than drawn.
  hw/arm/s5l8930_isp.c            the guest's ISP asks for a frame whether or
                                  not the phone has one, and does not know or
                                  care which camera its driver named: one
                                  mailbox serves whichever camera the guest is
                                  on. Each frame scales the phone's frame into
                                  the guest's mode, or draws the pattern when
                                  the phone has sent nothing (a desktop, a check
                                  boot). A frame size no sensor ever had is
                                  skipped instead of allocated and DMAed.
  hw/arm/s5l8930-isp-camera.h     the conversion itself, pure and testable: NV12
                                  in, NV12 or the ISP's 4:2:2 out, nearest
                                  neighbour, with its own unit test.
  contrib/macos-app/...           qemu_ios_camera_write/flush/sensor/state
                                  (API 2.5): the app's side of the hand-off, with
                                  no device and no guest memory involved.

Nothing here repairs, throttles, retries or waits: the old camera work's
timeouts, parking, fallbacks and extra devices are all gone. The ISP's timing is
the engine's own, untouched, because it is the part that already worked.

The first cut of this chose the picture's source from the sensor the kext named,
and the guest drew nothing at all -- a capture that waits for the guest to name a
camera is a capture that can wait for ever. That selection is gone.

diff --git a/contrib/macos-app/qemu-macos-extras.c b/contrib/macos-app/qemu-macos-extras.c
index d08cfe1..8a3b4db 100644
--- a/contrib/macos-app/qemu-macos-extras.c
+++ b/contrib/macos-app/qemu-macos-extras.c
@@ -39,6 +39,7 @@
 #include "qemu-macos-extras.h"
 #include "hw/misc/ios_vibrator.h"
 #include "hw/arm/host-mic.h"
+#include "hw/arm/host-camera.h"
 
 /*
  * system/main.c (replaced by qemu-ios-entry.c) defined this; ui/cocoa.m still
@@ -1111,3 +1112,41 @@ int qemu_ios_microphone_state(uint32_t *rate, uint32_t *queued, uint64_t *pushed
     }
     return state.capturing ? 1 : 0;
 }
+
+/*
+ * The phone's camera into the emulated device's ISP.
+ *
+ * The bridge is the engine's, not a device's: the app writes a frame whenever its camera has one,
+ * the ISP takes the newest when it draws the next one, and neither waits for the other. Nothing
+ * here touches guest memory or the device state, so it is safe to call from any thread, including
+ * before a machine exists -- a frame written early is just the first viewfinder picture.
+ */
+int qemu_ios_camera_write(int sensor, const void *nv12, uint32_t width, uint32_t height)
+{
+    return host_camera_write(sensor, nv12, width, height);
+}
+
+void qemu_ios_camera_flush(void)
+{
+    host_camera_flush();
+}
+
+int32_t qemu_ios_camera_sensor(void)
+{
+    return host_camera_active();
+}
+
+void qemu_ios_camera_state(int sensor, uint64_t *pushed, uint64_t *taken, uint64_t *replaced,
+                           uint32_t *width, uint32_t *height)
+{
+    unsigned w = 0, h = 0;
+
+    (void)sensor;   /* one mailbox serves whichever camera the guest is on */
+    host_camera_counts(pushed, taken, replaced, &w, &h);
+    if (width) {
+        *width = w;
+    }
+    if (height) {
+        *height = h;
+    }
+}
